# Humans as an Attack Vector

## Summary
Humans are often the weakest link in cybersecurity. 
Attackers exploit human psychology and emotions rather 
than technical vulnerabilities to gain access to systems 
and sensitive information.

## Key Concepts
- Social Engineering — Manipulating people into giving 
  up confidential information or access
- Phishing — Sending fake emails pretending to be 
  legitimate organizations to steal credentials
- Vishing — Same as phishing but done over the phone
- Pretexting — Creating a fake scenario to trick someone 
  into giving up information
- Baiting — Leaving infected USB drives or devices for 
  someone to find and plug in

## Why Humans Are Vulnerable
- We naturally trust authority figures
- We act differently under pressure or urgency
- We want to be helpful
- We are creatures of habit and routine

## How Attackers Exploit Humans
- Creating fake urgency like "your account will be deleted"
- Impersonating IT staff or management
- Exploiting emotions like fear, curiosity, or greed
- Building fake trust over time before attacking

## How Defenders Protect Against This
- Security awareness training for all staff
- Phishing simulation exercises
- Clear policies for verifying identities
- Teaching employees to slow down and verify requests

## My Key Takeaway
Technology alone cannot secure an organization because 
humans can be manipulated into bypassing even the 
strongest technical controls.

## Questions I Still Have
-How do we insure that the training is actually working. 
-What are all the ways an organization can be exploited through social engineering.
